{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1387444-origins-of-the-wyrm-in-fiction","id":1387444,"title":"origins of the wyrm in fiction","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-05-11T04:38:10-07:00","id":1387444,"title":"origins of the wyrm in fiction","content":"since alot of the WoD is inspired (or taken) from various sources in fiction,<br /> <br /> I was wondering if the triat and, in particular, the wyrm in its three forms (beast-of-war, defiler, eater-of-souls) had any origin (obvious or otherwise) in other works","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>-</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10998-pleiades","name":"Pleiades","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10998_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1844"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-11T05:38:30-07:00","id":1387458,"title":"","content":"Well there is the obvious Trimurti parallels of Divine Creator, Preserver, and Destroyer. We also tie that to western ideas of Wyrms/Dragons representing the Devil or great evils/scourges, such as the Dracula being a title meant to evoke the Devil in the form of a great Wyrm or the role of the Zmei in Russian lore. And a lot is the extrapolation of what if Animism was Real, and Mother Earth is Alive.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-11T08:05:47-07:00","id":1387495,"title":"","content":"Satan is often depicted as a Serpent or a Dragon. And in many different world mythologies, there is a hero (very often a storm god) that fights evil in the form of a great dragon.<br /> <br /> In terms of books, The Book of the Dun Cow by Walter Wangerin, Jr has an existential evil specifically called the Wyrm, and it has many elements reminiscent of Werewolf. I wouldn't be surprised if it was one of the inspirations for the game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-11T08:12:04-07:00","id":1387497,"title":"","content":"Oh interesting on the Dun Cow thing, I'll look it up!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-11T09:43:02-07:00","id":1387522,"title":"","content":"There's a recurring trope in a lot of ancient western mythologies of a certain divine order one one side (Ra and other champions of Ma'at, the Olympians, the Aesir, etc.) and forces of destruction wishing to tear that order down on the other (Apep, Typhon, Surtur, etc.). The idea of Satan as adversary of both God and Humanity, as well as associating him with dragons and other forces of chaos is in many ways a continuation of this theme. And because Werewolf drew a lot on pre-Christian cultures and mythologies, this carries through into the game. <br /> Animism is another of the game's big tropes, being the foundation of its cosmology and a big chunk of its setting. Everything has a spiritual aspect. And that includes things such as vices, negative emotions, corrupted places or objects, bad luck, and even the very concepts of evil, decay and corruption themselves. <br /> The other major influence is the works of HP Lovecraft and other writers of &quot;weird fiction&quot;, especially Lovecraft's concept of cosmicism. This cosmicist philosophy is sort of the antithesis of humanism, and of close kin to nihilism. Basically, human beings are largely insignificant in the greater scheme of things, and the universe is full of beings which are, at best, completely indifferent to humanity, but whose mere presence is enough to disrupt and even destroy the well being of anyone or anything caught in their path. <br /> (Combined, these form a cosmology I tend to refer to as Dark Animism; The world is spiritually alive, but often in a way that is uncaring or even hostile. Even the Garou have to bargain with spirits, and are only in a position to bargain with any strength because they are half-spirit themselves.)<br /> <br /> Generally, you can find a lot of the origins of aspects of the Wyrm and its various forces and incarnations in the works of Lovecraft, Robert E. Howard, Arthur Machen, Lord Dunsany, Robert Chambers, and Algernon Blackwood, as well as those influenced by them (Stephen King and Clive Barker among others). One of the major influences on the Black Spiral Dancers, especially their Pictish origins, is Howard's Bran Mac Morn stories. Also certain aspects of various 70s and 80s horror films. The 1979 film Prophecy and the 1984 film The Toxic Avenger were huge influences on the development of fomori.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-11T15:32:28-07:00","id":1387607,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Eldagusto</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1387444-origins-of-the-wyrm-in-fiction?p=1387497#post1387497\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh interesting on the Dun Cow thing, I'll look it up!</div> </div> </div> </div>It's an animal fable about the Apocalypse where the farmyard and woodland animals have to fight the animal equivalent of the anti-Christ that takes place before the coming of Man. There's a lot of elements you can probably use as inspiration for the spirits, the Umbra in general, and how the Red Talons might see the world. Since the main character is a Rooster, there's a lot you can use for packs with the Cockeral totem.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-11T16:39:14-07:00","id":1387615,"title":"","content":"I always get the sense that the Wyrm is the Eternal Champion (from Michael Moorecock) gone wrong. Specifically a cosmic version of Prince Gaynor the Damned from Corum and Elric.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/918-geeklord1","name":"geeklord1","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar918_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"87"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}